module sh.sysl.monocypher

import sh.sysl.monocypher.c
import sysl.slices.{as_ptr, as_mut_ptr}
import sysl.encoding.hex_string

// Cryptography, bound to Monocypher 4.0.3.
//
// The C sits in `c/` beside this file and is compiled as part of the library (`15 §7`), so **nothing
// has to be installed to use this package** and there is no `@link` anywhere here. A package that named a
// system library instead would work -- `--link-path` and `--include-path` are how a build says where
// one lives -- but it would then need that library present, in a compatible version, on every machine
// that compiled it. Carrying the source needs nothing of the machine at all, which is the property
// that lets this build for a freestanding target as readily as for a host.
//
// **Monocypher includes `<stddef.h>` and `<stdint.h>` and nothing else.** It allocates nothing, opens
// nothing, and asks the operating system for nothing, which is why `package.hocon` names no
// capability and why this builds for a freestanding target. Every function below writes into storage
// the caller already has.
//
// What crosses the boundary is a pointer and a length, in both directions. There is **no shim `.c`
// file** -- unusual for a binding, and worth saying why: every function bound here takes `uint8_t *`
// and `size_t` and returns `int` or nothing, so there is no `#define` to read, no struct whose layout
// only the header knows, and nothing for a shim to translate. `sysl/bindings/regex` needs one because
// `sizeof(regex_t)` and `REG_EXTENDED` exist; Monocypher's public surface has no such thing.
//
// **An empty message is a real input to every function here** -- BLAKE2b of the empty string is a
// published test vector, and sealing an empty plaintext under a key is an ordinary thing to do -- so
// every slice reaches C through `sysl.slices.as_ptr`, which answers `null` for an empty one rather
// than trapping the way `&b[0]` would. This file used to carry its own version of that function,
// handing back a scratch byte instead of `null`; the library's is the better answer here for a reason
// specific to Monocypher, and worth stating because it does not generalise. **`as_ptr`'s one
// documented cost is that ISO C leaves `memcpy(dst, NULL, 0)` undefined -- and Monocypher calls
// `memcpy` nowhere at all.** It copies with explicit loops, deliberately, so that it needs no C
// library; a length of zero is a loop that does not run, and there is no libc function to hand a null
// to. A binding whose C does use `memcpy` has to think about this again.
//
// **This binding supplies no randomness, and that is Monocypher's design rather than an omission.**
// Every secret below is an input. A caller generating a real key must bring 32 bytes from the
// platform's entropy source; `sysl.fs` reading `/dev/urandom` is the hosted answer and a freestanding
// program has to say what its own source is. A key made from anything predictable is not a key, and a
// binding that quietly supplied one would be worse than a binding that supplies none.

// The sizes the algorithms are defined at. They are `const` rather than written at each call so that
// a caller sizing a buffer and this file checking it cannot come to disagree.
//
// **These are NOT asked for with `c const`, and the reason is that there is nothing to ask.**
// `monocypher.h` defines no size macros: the numbers are written into the array parameters —
// `key[32]`, `signature[64]` — where they are documentation to a reader and nothing at all to the
// compiler, since C decays such a parameter to a pointer. The only `#define`s in either header are
// Argon2's three variants, which this binding does not bind.
//
// So they come from the algorithms' own specifications rather than from a header: an X25519 key is 32
// bytes because Curve25519 is a 255-bit curve. That is the same case as st7796's datasheet command
// bytes, and the opposite of termbox2's or qcbor's `#define`s — where a header does define a constant,
// asking the C compiler for it is the rule (`15 §7`).
const key_size: usize = 32
const nonce_size: usize = 24
const mac_size: usize = 16
const public_key_size: usize = 32
const secret_key_size: usize = 64
const signature_size: usize = 64
const seed_size: usize = 32
const shared_secret_size: usize = 32
const max_hash_size: usize = 64
const sha512_size: usize = 64

// BLAKE2b, into a digest buffer whose length chooses the digest size.
//
// The size is the buffer's rather than a parameter because those are the same number, and a function
// taking both would have two ways to be told and one way to be wrong. 64 bytes is the full digest;
// 32 is the common choice where a shorter one will do.
blake2b(hash: []u8, message: []const u8)
    require hash.len >= 1 && hash.len <= max_hash_size, "a digest is 1 to 64 bytes"

    c.blake2b(&hash[0], hash.len, as_ptr(message), message.len)
end blake2b

// BLAKE2b with a key, which is how it is used as a MAC.
//
// This is the reason BLAKE2b needs no HMAC construction wrapped around it: the key goes into the
// hash itself. A key may be up to 64 bytes and may be empty, in which case this is `blake2b`.
blake2b_keyed(hash: []u8, key: []const u8, message: []const u8)
    require hash.len >= 1 && hash.len <= max_hash_size, "a digest is 1 to 64 bytes"
    require key.len <= max_hash_size, "a BLAKE2b key is at most 64 bytes"

    c.blake2b_keyed(&hash[0], hash.len, as_ptr(key), key.len,
                    as_ptr(message), message.len)
end blake2b_keyed

// Encrypt and authenticate, with XChaCha20-Poly1305.
//
// `cipher` receives exactly as many bytes as `plain` has, and `mac` receives the 16-byte tag that
// proves both the ciphertext and `ad` are untouched. **`ad` is authenticated and not encrypted** --
// it is for the envelope a recipient must read before it can decrypt, a message number or a routing
// header, and it may be empty.
//
// **The nonce must never repeat under one key.** That is the whole discipline this construction asks
// of a caller and the one it cannot check: two messages sealed under the same key and nonce leak
// their plaintexts to anyone holding both. 24 bytes is wide enough that a random nonce per message is
// safe, which is the reason to prefer this over the 8- and 12-byte variants Monocypher also has.
lock(cipher: []u8, mac: []u8, key: []const u8, nonce: []const u8, ad: []const u8, plain: []const u8)
    require key.len == key_size, "an XChaCha20-Poly1305 key is 32 bytes"
    require nonce.len == nonce_size, "an XChaCha20 nonce is 24 bytes"
    require mac.len == mac_size, "a Poly1305 tag is 16 bytes"
    require cipher.len == plain.len, "the ciphertext is exactly as long as the plaintext"

    c.aead_lock(as_mut_ptr(cipher), &mac[0], &key[0], &nonce[0],
                as_ptr(ad), ad.len, as_ptr(plain), plain.len)
end lock

// Verify and decrypt. `false` means the message is not the one that was sealed -- a wrong key, a
// wrong nonce, altered ciphertext, or altered `ad` -- and `plain` is wiped rather than left holding
// whatever the wrong key produced.
//
// **The answer is a `bool` and not a `Result` because there is only one failure and nothing to say
// about it.** Which of the four went wrong is exactly what an attacker would like to be told, and a
// recipient does the same thing in every case: discard the message.
unlock(plain: []u8, mac: []const u8, key: []const u8, nonce: []const u8, ad: []const u8,
       cipher: []const u8) -> bool
    require key.len == key_size, "an XChaCha20-Poly1305 key is 32 bytes"
    require nonce.len == nonce_size, "an XChaCha20 nonce is 24 bytes"
    require mac.len == mac_size, "a Poly1305 tag is 16 bytes"
    require plain.len == cipher.len, "the plaintext is exactly as long as the ciphertext"

    c.aead_unlock(as_mut_ptr(plain), &mac[0], &key[0], &nonce[0],
                  as_ptr(ad), ad.len, as_ptr(cipher), cipher.len) == 0
end unlock

// The X25519 public key that goes with a secret key, for key exchange.
//
// The secret key is 32 bytes and every 32 bytes is a valid one, so this is where a caller's random
// bytes become a key pair.
x25519_public_key(public_key: []u8, secret_key: []const u8)
    require public_key.len == public_key_size, "an X25519 public key is 32 bytes"
    require secret_key.len == key_size, "an X25519 secret key is 32 bytes"

    c.x25519_public_key(&public_key[0], &secret_key[0])
end x25519_public_key

// The shared secret two parties reach from one's secret key and the other's public key.
//
// **The result is not a key and must not be used as one.** It is a curve point, and the low bits of
// a curve point are not uniformly distributed; hash it first. `blake2b` over this buffer is the
// usual answer, and it is also where a transcript of who was talking to whom belongs.
x25519(shared: []u8, your_secret_key: []const u8, their_public_key: []const u8)
    require shared.len == shared_secret_size, "an X25519 shared secret is 32 bytes"
    require your_secret_key.len == key_size, "an X25519 secret key is 32 bytes"
    require their_public_key.len == public_key_size, "an X25519 public key is 32 bytes"

    c.x25519(&shared[0], &your_secret_key[0], &their_public_key[0])
end x25519

// **THE TWO SIGNATURE SCHEMES BELOW ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE, AND CHOOSING WRONG IS SILENT.**
//
// Both are EdDSA over curve25519 and both produce 64 bytes, so nothing about the types tells them
// apart -- but they hash with different functions, so a signature made by one is rejected by the
// other with no diagnostic beyond `false`.
//
//   - `ed25519_sign` / `ed25519_check` hash with **SHA-512**. This is Ed25519 as RFC 8032 specifies
//     it, and it is what every other implementation in the world means by the name. **Use these
//     unless you have a reason not to.**
//   - `eddsa_sign` / `eddsa_check` hash with **BLAKE2b**. Monocypher's own variant: smaller, because
//     a program already using BLAKE2b needs no second hash compiled in, and interoperable with
//     nothing but Monocypher. Choose it only for both ends of a link you control.
//
// The `x25519` key exchange above has no such split and is the same on either side.

// An EdDSA-BLAKE2b signing key pair, derived from a 32-byte seed.
//
// **The seed is wiped by this call**, which is why it is `[]u8` and not `[]const u8`. Monocypher
// destroys it deliberately: the seed reconstructs the secret key, so a copy left in the caller's
// buffer is a second secret to protect. Bring the seed from the platform's entropy source.
eddsa_key_pair(secret_key: []u8, public_key: []u8, seed: []u8)
    require secret_key.len == secret_key_size, "an EdDSA secret key is 64 bytes"
    require public_key.len == public_key_size, "an EdDSA public key is 32 bytes"
    require seed.len == seed_size, "an EdDSA seed is 32 bytes"

    c.eddsa_key_pair(&secret_key[0], &public_key[0], &seed[0])
end eddsa_key_pair

// Sign a message with EdDSA-BLAKE2b. The signature is 64 bytes and the message may be empty.
eddsa_sign(signature: []u8, secret_key: []const u8, message: []const u8)
    require signature.len == signature_size, "an EdDSA signature is 64 bytes"
    require secret_key.len == secret_key_size, "an EdDSA secret key is 64 bytes"

    c.eddsa_sign(&signature[0], &secret_key[0], as_ptr(message), message.len)
end eddsa_sign

// Whether an EdDSA-BLAKE2b signature is the one that key made over that message.
eddsa_check(signature: []const u8, public_key: []const u8, message: []const u8) -> bool
    require signature.len == signature_size, "an EdDSA signature is 64 bytes"
    require public_key.len == public_key_size, "an EdDSA public key is 32 bytes"

    c.eddsa_check(&signature[0], &public_key[0], as_ptr(message), message.len) == 0
end eddsa_check

// An Ed25519 signing key pair, derived from a 32-byte seed. The seed is wiped, as above.
//
// **The 32 bytes of the seed are the private key** as RFC 8032 uses the word. The 64-byte secret key
// here is the expanded form -- the seed's hash, with the public key appended -- which is what signing
// actually reads. Storing the seed and re-deriving is equivalent to storing the secret key.
ed25519_key_pair(secret_key: []u8, public_key: []u8, seed: []u8)
    require secret_key.len == secret_key_size, "an Ed25519 secret key is 64 bytes"
    require public_key.len == public_key_size, "an Ed25519 public key is 32 bytes"
    require seed.len == seed_size, "an Ed25519 seed is 32 bytes"

    c.ed25519_key_pair(&secret_key[0], &public_key[0], &seed[0])
end ed25519_key_pair

// Sign a message with Ed25519 (RFC 8032). This is the one that interoperates.
ed25519_sign(signature: []u8, secret_key: []const u8, message: []const u8)
    require signature.len == signature_size, "an Ed25519 signature is 64 bytes"
    require secret_key.len == secret_key_size, "an Ed25519 secret key is 64 bytes"

    c.ed25519_sign(&signature[0], &secret_key[0], as_ptr(message), message.len)
end ed25519_sign

// Whether an Ed25519 signature is the one that key made over that message.
ed25519_check(signature: []const u8, public_key: []const u8, message: []const u8) -> bool
    require signature.len == signature_size, "an Ed25519 signature is 64 bytes"
    require public_key.len == public_key_size, "an Ed25519 public key is 32 bytes"

    c.ed25519_check(&signature[0], &public_key[0], as_ptr(message), message.len) == 0
end ed25519_check

// SHA-512, into a 64-byte buffer.
//
// It is here because Ed25519 needs it and it would be compiled in anyway, and because a program
// talking to something that is not sysl usually needs the hash everything else already speaks.
// **For a new protocol prefer `blake2b`**: it is faster, it takes a key without an HMAC construction
// around it, and it has no length-extension weakness to design around.
sha512(hash: []u8, message: []const u8)
    require hash.len == sha512_size, "a SHA-512 digest is 64 bytes"

    c.sha512(&hash[0], as_ptr(message), message.len)
end sha512

// Whether two secrets are equal, in time that does not depend on where they differ.
//
// **`a == b` is the wrong way to compare a MAC or a digest.** An ordinary comparison stops at the
// first differing byte, and how long it took is a measurement an attacker can make -- enough, over
// many attempts, to find a valid tag a byte at a time. This reads both buffers entirely, every time.
//
// 16, 32 and 64 bytes are the lengths Monocypher offers, because they are the sizes of the things
// worth comparing this way: a Poly1305 tag, a digest or public key, a signature.
equal(a: []const u8, b: []const u8) -> bool
    require a.len == b.len, "two buffers of different lengths are not the same secret"
    require a.len == mac_size || a.len == key_size || a.len == signature_size, "16, 32 or 64 bytes"

    if a.len == mac_size then
        c.verify16(&a[0], &b[0]) == 0
    elif a.len == key_size then
        c.verify32(&a[0], &b[0]) == 0
    else
        c.verify64(&a[0], &b[0]) == 0
end equal

// Overwrite a buffer holding a secret with zeroes.
//
// This exists because the obvious loop does not survive the optimizer: a compiler that can see the
// buffer is never read again is entitled to delete the writes, and every one of them does. Monocypher
// writes through a volatile pointer, which it may not remove.
wipe(secret: []u8)
    if secret.len > 0 then c.wipe(&secret[0], secret.len)
end wipe

// Bytes as lowercase hexadecimal.
//
// **Not Monocypher's, and not cryptography.** It is here because everything above answers in bytes
// and every way of checking the answer -- a test vector, a log line, a value pasted into an issue --
// is hexadecimal. A binding whose entire output were unprintable would be one every caller had to
// write this for.
//
// **It is the library's renderer, and it used to be a copy.** The paragraph above once continued
// "and sysl's library has no renderer for a byte string", which was true when it was written and is
// not now: `sysl.encoding.hex_string` says the same thing, in lower case, and this is the name it
// answers to here. The name stays because it is what a caller of this package reaches for beside
// `lock` and `sign`, and because `argon2.sysl` next door already reaches into the same module for
// base64.
//
// It is also the one function in this file that allocates, since it builds a `string`. The
// cryptography does not.
hex(bytes: []const u8) -> string = hex_string(bytes)
